**New evidence**
Sharif’s coalition and social-safety team framed welfare as a governing objective early in 2022, with explicit references to poverty reduction, human capital, women, health, education, malnutrition, and data-driven delivery.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"SCO International Forum for Poverty Alleviation is a great effort, Shazia Marri","publisher":"PID / Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-05-26","url":"https://pid.gov.pk/site/press_detail/19910","claim":"The poverty-alleviation ministry said the present government was using data, technology, financial inclusion, women’s empowerment, human-capital formation, and reduced barriers to health and education to build a welfare state.","locator":"keynote address text, 26 May 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct government statement by the responsible minister in 2022; rhetoric is explicit, though implementation still needs corroboration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within Sharif’s 2022 ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sharif-led federal government through its poverty-alleviation ministry; it reflects stated policy intent more than measurable delivery.","contrary_evidence":["This is mainly an intent statement and does not by itself prove implementation or outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

The federal health agenda in mid-2022 included a World Bank-financed push to widen equitable access to primary care, especially for women and children in underserved districts.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Supports Primary Healthcare and Universal Coverage to Strengthen Human Capital in Pakistan","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/06/-world-bank-supports-primary-healthcare-and-universal-coverage-to-strengthen-human-capital-in-pakistan","claim":"The World Bank approved $258 million for Pakistan’s National Health Support Program to strengthen primary health care and accelerate universal health coverage, with emphasis on communities lagging behind and on access for children and women during pregnancy and childbirth.","locator":"press release lead and program summary","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with concrete financing and program design details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and under the Sharif government.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sharif-led government’s health policy environment, but financed and implemented with provincial and donor participation.","contrary_evidence":["World Bank financing is not the same as Sharif personally designing or executing the program."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

Women’s and maternal nutrition was another explicit policy focus, again with an equity and service-delivery framing rather than a generic growth narrative.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan Maternal Nutrition Strategy 2022-27","publisher":"UNICEF Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/pakistan/reports/pakistan-maternal-nutrition-strategy-2022-27","claim":"UNICEF and the Government of Pakistan set a strategy to protect and promote diets, practices, and services supporting women’s health and well-being, with evidence-based and equity-based nutrition programmes at scale for preconception, pregnancy, and postpartum periods.","locator":"Highlights and strategy objectives","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UNICEF-government strategy document with clear program goals and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sharif-era federal government working with UNICEF; implementation was shared with health systems and provinces.","contrary_evidence":["This is a strategy document rather than proof of nationwide delivery in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8"]}

Health-system modernization was also being piloted through a basic-package approach that aimed at more equitable access and stronger provincial localization.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Towards sustainable financing for PHC and UHC: The piloting of Essential Package of Health Services in Pakistan","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/bringing-support-phc-sustainable-health-financing-together-pakistan","claim":"WHO said Pakistan had approved a limited national Essential Package of Health Services for immediate district-level implementation and had localized the package into provincial plans and budgets in all provinces except Punjab by May 2022.","locator":"overview and 'Moving planning to results' sections","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO feature story describing a concrete health-policy pilot and the status of localization in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Pakistan health system under the Sharif government; WHO and donors were co-implementers, so attribution is shared.","contrary_evidence":["The story describes piloting and partial localization, not full national implementation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

A separate poverty-alleviation initiative was described as part of the prime minister’s vision and targeted poorer households with interest-free loans.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PR No.53","publisher":"PID / Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-05-14","url":"https://pid.gov.pk/site/press_detail/19807","claim":"The finance minister said the Kamyab Pakistan Program was consistent with Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif’s vision to improve living standards and would provide interest-free loans to poorer segments through a bottom-up approach.","locator":"meeting summary, 14 May 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct government press release naming the program, purpose, and beneficiary design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and Sharif’s first months in office.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sharif-led federal government through the finance ministry; this is an intent-and-design source more than a delivery audit.","contrary_evidence":["The release does not establish whether the program materially improved incomes in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.10"]}

When floods struck, Sharif personally directed fast cash relief through BISP with electronic transfer and a transparent process.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif directed to ensure the provision of Rs. 50,000 relief to each flood affected family through a transparent process within the three days","publisher":"PID / Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-08-15","url":"https://pid.gov.pk/site/press_detail/20561","claim":"The prime minister directed that every flood-affected family receive Rs. 50,000 within three days, with BISP cash relief delivered under NDMA supervision and via electronic transfer so deserving people would get relief.","locator":"PR No. 135, 15 August 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PM instruction recorded in the official press release with concrete amount, mechanism, and delivery timeline.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Shehbaz Sharif as prime minister; operational delivery still depended on BISP, NDMA, and provincial machinery.","contrary_evidence":["This was emergency relief, not a durable reform of ordinary welfare provision."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.10"]}

The flood-relief envelope was expanded soon afterward, with broader coverage and larger disbursements through BISP.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Federal Government has increased the amount of flood relief package to Rupees 70 billion","publisher":"Benazir Income Support Programme","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://bisp.gov.pk/NewsDetail/NDhmMGJjMTEtYjAyMi00NGM4LWFjN2QtMDdmZTM4ZGFjNzdj","claim":"BISP said the federal government increased the flood-relief cash package from Rs. 28 billion to Rs. 70 billion and was distributing Rs. 25,000 per family transparently, including in Gilgit-Baltistan.","locator":"23 Sep 2022 news item","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Government social-protection agency reporting specific package size, transfer amount, and coverage expansion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and the flood response window.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sharif-led federal government, though BISP executed the disbursements and provincial/local conditions shaped access.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is crisis relief rather than evidence of a strengthened ordinary welfare system."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.10"]}

By year-end, Sharif’s government launched a formal recovery framework built around resilience and inclusive reconstruction.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PR No. 177 PRIME MINISTER OF PAKISTAN MUHAMMAD SHEHBAZ SHARIF LAUNCHES THE RESILIENT, RECOVERY, REHABILITATION AND RECONSTRUCTION FRAMEWORK (4RF) FOR PAKISTAN FLOODS","publisher":"PID / Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-12-21","url":"https://pid.gov.pk/site/press_detail/21692","claim":"Shehbaz Sharif launched the 4RF for Pakistan Floods 2022, a short-, medium-, and long-term climate-resilient recovery and reconstruction framework based on the PDNA and presented as an inclusive whole-of-Pakistan approach.","locator":"PR No. 177, 21 December 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PM launch notice with concrete policy architecture and timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sharif, though the framework itself was prepared with ministries, provinces, and development partners.","contrary_evidence":["A framework is not the same as measured reconstruction completion."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

Humanitarian food and livelihoods support also expanded in response to the flood shock, with the government described as leading the coordination.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WFP steps up humanitarian support to government relief efforts as Pakistan reels from devastating floods","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/wfp-steps-humanitarian-support-government-relief-efforts-pakistan-reels-devastating-floods","claim":"WFP said it was expanding food assistance, malnutrition prevention, and livelihoods support for flood-affected families while the Government of Pakistan, through NDMA, was leading the response and coordinating relief.","locator":"lead and situation sections","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WFP operational report with explicit role assignment and concrete assistance categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sharif government as the leading national authority, but the response was multiactor and heavily dependent on humanitarian partners.","contrary_evidence":["The same source underscores that the disaster had overwhelmed domestic capacity and required external support."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

WHO’s 2022 Pakistan crisis materials show both the scale of the damage and the concrete health delivery response.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Impact in 2022: Pakistan","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.who.int/emergencies/funding/health-emergency-appeals/2022/impact-in-2022/pakistan","claim":"WHO said Pakistan’s floods damaged or destroyed about 10 percent of health facilities, left more than eight million people needing urgent health assistance, and prompted a measles-rubella vaccination campaign that reached 1.8 million children across 38 districts.","locator":"Pakistan impact page overview","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO operational summary with concrete response outputs and damage estimates.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood-response period.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly crisis-response context rather than a direct PM decision, but the government was the national counterpart for the health response.","contrary_evidence":["The same source makes clear that the disaster severely disrupted health services and created major unmet need."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

Floods also caused severe education disruption, and UNICEF responded with temporary learning centres and school rehabilitation.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Schools for more than 2 million children in Pakistan remain inaccessible due to devastating floods","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/schools-more-2-million-children-pakistan-remain-inaccessible-due-devastating-floods","claim":"UNICEF said more than 2 million children remained out of school after floods damaged or destroyed nearly 27,000 schools and that it had established more than 500 temporary learning centres and supported teachers and children with supplies.","locator":"press release body and notes for editors","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF release with specific counts and response measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is mostly crisis-context and partner response, not a PM-specific claim, but it defines the child-welfare stakes of the Sharif period.","contrary_evidence":["The source highlights continuing educational deprivation rather than a clean welfare improvement."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

WHO’s September emergency statement makes clear how much of the welfare picture was dominated by flood-induced health-system damage and the need for coordinated response.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Director-General's statement on Pakistan","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022-09-17","url":"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-09-2022-who-director-general-s-statement-on-pakistan--17-09-2022","claim":"WHO said flooded health centres, unsafe water, damaged supplies, and disrupted access to care threatened more unsafe births and missed vaccinations, while WHO and partners were working with the government to set up temporary facilities and resupply medicines.","locator":"statement text","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WHO statement describing crisis conditions and response mechanics in real time.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"Mainly crisis-response context with government partnership, not a direct PM policy act.","contrary_evidence":["The statement shows the depth of ongoing service disruption and unmet need."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Women and girls were singled out as especially exposed to the flood shock, especially because maternal health services were disrupted.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Women and girls bearing the brunt of the Pakistan Monsoon floods","publisher":"UNFPA Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://pakistan.unfpa.org/en/news/women-and-girls-bearing-brunt-pakistan-monsoon-floods","claim":"UNFPA said the floods left roughly 6.4 million people needing humanitarian assistance, including about 1.6 million women of childbearing age, and estimated that nearly 650,000 pregnant women would need maternal health services.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNFPA crisis assessment with concrete estimates for vulnerable groups.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"This is crisis context rather than a direct PM choice, but it defines the vulnerable-group burden facing the Sharif government.","contrary_evidence":["The source is mostly about need and risk, not evidence of successful government protection."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

The World Bank’s flood assessment pushed an explicitly equity-oriented recovery frame and warned of deep poverty impacts.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan: Flood Damages and Economic Losses Over USD 30 billion and Reconstruction Needs Over USD 16 billion - New Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/28/pakistan-flood-damages-and-economic-losses-over-usd-30-billion-and-reconstruction-needs-over-usd-16-billion-new-assessme","claim":"The PDNA called for recovery based on the principles of poor first, transparency, inclusion, and climate resilience, and said the floods affected 33 million people and could push 8.4 to 9.1 million more people below the poverty line.","locator":"press release lead and human-impact section","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint World Bank assessment using formal post-disaster methodology and explicit social-impact estimates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and essential for end-state evaluation.","ruler_attribution":"The disaster was external, but the recovery framework and distributional response were government responsibilities under Sharif.","contrary_evidence":["This source is dominated by shock effects rather than successful policy delivery."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

At the end of 2022, the government also approved province-specific flood recovery projects that explicitly targeted mothers, children, and vulnerable communities.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan: World Bank Approves $1.692 Billion for 5 Projects to Support the People of Sindh in the Flood-Affected Areas","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/19/pakistan-world-bank-approves-1-692-billion-for-5-projects-to-support-the-people-of-sindh-in-the-flood-affected-areas","claim":"The World Bank approved five projects for flood-affected Sindh, including social protection delivery, conditional cash transfers for 1.3 million mothers and children, and health services for women, girls, and children in flood-affected settlements.","locator":"press release body, especially project descriptions","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary financing announcement with specific beneficiary groups and delivery mechanisms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to the end-of-year welfare trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sharif government’s recovery agenda, though financed and structured jointly with the World Bank and implemented at provincial level.","contrary_evidence":["Province-specific recovery financing does not by itself show nationwide welfare improvement by year-end."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PID/government releases, World Bank assessments and program notes, WHO crisis pages and statements, UNICEF flood and nutrition releases, WFP emergency updates, UNFPA flood coverage, and BISP materials. I retained 14 new URLs and reused 6 earlier evidence items that genuinely fit the 6B lenses.

I excluded duplicate syndications of the same flood story, later retrospective explainers that did not add target-period evidence, generic baseline pages that lacked a 2022 ruler-period choice, and current-program pages that were useful background but not as clean for the 2022 target window. I also avoided reusing the same URL twice as both reused and new evidence.

**Remaining gaps**
The dossier is strongest on flood-driven crisis response, maternal/child welfare, and the design of social-protection and health programs. The main gaps are:
- Direct evidence that Sharif personally drove routine, non-crisis improvements in health, education, nutrition, or housing beyond public launches and emergency relief.
- Independent evaluations of whether the 2022 relief and recovery measures actually improved outcomes for households by year-end.
- More precise attribution between federal direction, provincial execution, and donor financing.
- Better 2022 evidence on school re-entry, learning recovery, and longer-run nutrition impacts after the floods.

More search would help, especially on budget execution, independent program evaluations, and district-level outcome data.
